{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A truncation through a JSON-escaped credential can leave sensitive content in stored output and model input."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Dropping a blank line prevents matching a multiline credential, exposing its private lines in failure excerpts."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"outage","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A gate emitting sufficiently large diagnostic lines can exhaust daemon memory because retained lines have no byte bound."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"secret","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Redacting an overlapping shorter credential first can expose the remaining lines of a multiline credential."},{"id":"C5","consequence":"diag","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Fields containing the literal redaction marker are entirely withheld, hiding useful diagnostics."},{"id":"C7","consequence":"state","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Loading and saving a run can permanently remove literal terminal escape sequences from persisted instructions."}]}